---
// The 5-part tutorial outline, following the EMNLP 2026 proposal. Part 1 sets
// up the agent development loop; Parts 2 to 5 each follow the same arc: failure
// patterns, diagnostics, fixes, and (where the proposal lists them) open
// challenges. Safety is threaded throughout rather than being a standalone part.

// Column layout for Parts 2 to 5.
const ARC = [
  { key: 'failures', label: 'Failure patterns', cls: 'col-fail' },
  { key: 'diagnostics', label: 'Diagnostics', cls: 'col-diag' },
  { key: 'fixes', label: 'Fixes', cls: 'col-fix' },
];

const parts = [
  {
    n: 'Part 1',
    title: 'The Development Loop of AI Agents',
    minutes: 10,
    lead: 'All presenters',
    summary:
      'An agent is not a single LLM call but a closed loop: it perceives, plans, acts through tools, and observes over many steps while persisting state in memory. We survey the landscape and lay out a build lifecycle that maps onto the rest of the tutorial.',
    columns: [
      {
        label: 'Agent landscape',
        cls: 'col-info',
        items: [
          'Open-source personal assistants (OpenClaw, Hermes Agent)',
          'Coding agents (Devin, Claude Code)',
          'Browser and computer-use agents (OpenAI Operator, ChatGPT Agent, Claude Computer Use)',
          'Deep-research agents (DR Tulu)',
          'Embodied agents (π*0.6)',
        ],
      },
      {
        label: 'Build lifecycle',
        cls: 'col-info',
        items: [
          'Scaffold and tools for multi-turn trajectories (Part 2)',
          'Post-training with SFT and RL (Part 3)',
          'Evaluation and red teaming beyond single-turn benchmarks (Part 4)',
          'Memory, skills, and test-time adaptation (Part 5)',
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    n: 'Part 2',
    title: 'Agent Environments and Infrastructure',
    minutes: 40,
    lead: 'Zoey Sha Li',
    summary:
      'Multi-turn interaction and tool use define the agent trajectory: model-generated actions interleaved with environment-generated observations. Standard RL frameworks treat a rollout as a single forward pass, so multi-turn agents need an orchestration layer to run rollouts, dispatch tools, and manage sandboxes.',
    failures: [
      'Token misattribution: environment and tool tokens leak into the loss, or re-tokenization at turn boundaries misaligns the loss mask and cached log-probs',
      'Low-probability tool tokens trigger gradient-norm spikes that precede reward collapse',
      'Overlong and timed-out trajectories wrongly scored as task failures',
    ],
    diagnostics: [
      'Early warnings before collapse: falling reward standard deviation and gradient-norm spikes',
      'Mask-alignment and log-prob consistency checks for re-tokenization drift',
      'Per-turn inspection to locate void or unproductive turns',
    ],
    fixes: [
      'Mask environment and tool tokens so the policy trains only on what it controls',
      'Token-in-token-out trajectories with importance-sampling correction for training-inference and off-policy mismatch',
      'Filter void turns (SimpleTIR); mask rather than penalize overlong rollouts (DAPO)',
    ],
    challenges: [
      'Composable, transferable environment abstractions',
      'Automatic synthesis of real-world environments at scale (Agent-World)',
      'The harness as a safety surface once agents read untrusted observations and call external tools (GuardAgent)',
    ],
  },
  {
    n: 'Part 3',
    title: 'Training Dynamics',
    minutes: 40,
    lead: 'Zihan Wang',
    summary:
      'Three failures of RL training: collapse under verifiable rewards, where the policy prematurely converges to a narrow set of behaviors; reward hacking under learned rewards, where it exploits an imperfect proxy; and the zero-gradient barrier, where no rollout succeeds and there is nothing to reinforce.',
    failures: [
      'Collapse under verifiable rewards: entropy, KL mode, Echo Trap, format, and reasoning collapse, each invisible to the previous diagnostic',
      'Reward hacking under learned rewards: reward-model overoptimization and RLHF-induced diversity loss',
      'Zero-gradient barrier: the task is too hard, the pass rate is zero, and policy gradients have nothing to reinforce',
    ],
    diagnostics: [
      'Collapse-aware proxies that flag collapse before reward drops (RAGEN-2)',
      'A widening proxy vs true reward gap; behavioral probes that catch exploits reward curves miss',
      'Zero within-group reward variance; pass@1 vs pass@k divergence',
    ],
    fixes: [
      'Entropy-preserving clipping (DAPO) and uncertainty-guided exploration control (T²PO)',
      'Constrained RLHF objectives that contain overoptimization',
      'External signal with decreasing teacher information: off-policy traces (LUFFY), prefix-guided exploration (POPE), abstract hints (NuRL), staged dense-to-binary rewards',
    ],
  },
  {
    n: 'Part 4',
    title: 'Evaluation and Red Teaming',
    minutes: 40,
    lead: 'Manling Li',
    summary:
      'Leaderboard scores routinely fail to measure what we think. We show where static benchmarks break, how closed-loop evaluation restores signal, and how red teaming probes the attack surfaces flagged in Parts 2, 3, and 5.',
    failures: [
      'Contamination: test instances leak into training, so the score reflects recall',
      'Saturation: a benchmark stops discriminating as models reach its ceiling (SWE-bench)',
      'Construct invalidity: the benchmark does not isolate the intended ability',
      'Harness variance: the same backbone scores differently across scaffolds and tools',
      'Attack surfaces: prompt injection, reward hacking under safety constraints, memory poisoning',
    ],
    diagnostics: [
      'Black-box tests for test-set contamination',
      'Harness-controlled comparisons of one backbone across scaffolds (Harness-Bench)',
      'The attack surface as a measurable axis under adversarial inputs (Agent Security Bench)',
    ],
    fixes: [
      'Dynamic, live, and asynchronous evaluation that resists memorization and restores headroom (ARE/Gaia2, Online-Mind2Web)',
      'Social, embodied, and out-of-sync recovery evaluation (SOTOPIA, SyncMind)',
      'Verifiable safety-policy reasoning as a runtime defense (ShieldAgent)',
    ],
  },
  {
    n: 'Part 5',
    title: 'Test-Time Scaling and Adaptation',
    minutes: 40,
    lead: 'Hejie Cui',
    summary:
      'Two failures of deployment-time improvement: test-time scaling, where more inference compute stops helping, and memory and skills, where the state an agent accumulates degrades rather than aids it.',
    failures: [
      'Inverse-U test-time scaling: accuracy peaks, then degrades into over-reasoning loops, self-doubt loops, and reasoning collapse at long horizons',
      'Memory failures by tier: context saturation, drift as facts grow stale, and poisoning that turns the store into an attack vector',
      'Skill failures in self-authored, cached skills: generation errors, overgeneralization, and a verification gap',
    ],
    diagnostics: [
      'Accuracy vs token-budget and accuracy vs turn curves that expose the peak',
      'Compression ratio paired with information-retention and functional-equivalence probes',
      'Retrieval precision and recall',
    ],
    fixes: [
      'Budget-aware early stopping (BAGEN) and stepwise length penalties (SWAP)',
      'Compaction-adapted reasoning (Reasoning Cache, Free())',
      'Learned compaction (MEM1) and memory-as-tool stores for facts and skill workflows (Mem0, AWM)',
      'Sub-agent delegation (Context-Folding) and decoupled asynchronous compression (CoMem)',
    ],
    challenges: [
      'When to trigger compression',
      'Adversarial robustness of memory to poisoned or contradictory context',
      'Restraint in personalization',
      'Guardrail decay under prolonged interaction (red-teamed in Part 4)',
    ],
  },
];

// Part 1 supplies its own columns; the rest use the failure/diagnostic/fix arc.
const outline = parts.map((p) => ({
  ...p,
  cols: p.columns ?? ARC.map((c) => ({ label: c.label, cls: c.cls, items: p[c.key] })),
}));
---
<section id="outline" class="content-section">
  <div class="section-container">
    <h2 class="section-title">Tutorial Outline</h2>

    <div class="parts">
      {outline.map((p) => (
        <article class="part">
          <header class="part-header">
            <div class="part-tag">
              <span class="part-n">{p.n}</span>
              <span class="part-time">{p.minutes} min</span>
              <span class="part-lead">{p.lead}</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="part-title">{p.title}</h3>
            {p.summary && <p class="part-summary">{p.summary}</p>}
          </header>

          <div class="part-cols" style={`--cols: ${p.cols.length}`}>
            {p.cols.map((c) => (
              <div class={`col ${c.cls}`}>
                <h4>{c.label}</h4>
                <ul>{c.items.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>

          {p.challenges && (
            <div class="part-challenges">
              <h4>Open challenges</h4>
              <ul>{p.challenges.map((x) => <li>{x}</li>)}</ul>
            </div>
          )}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  #outline {
    background: white;
    padding: 4rem 1rem;
  }

  .section-container {
    max-width: var(--page-max-width, 1100px);
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .section-title {
    font-size: 2.4rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #2a2f6c;
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .parts {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }

  .part {
    background: white;
    border-radius: 14px;
    border: 1px solid #e6e8ef;
    overflow: hidden;
    transition: box-shadow 0.2s ease, transform 0.2s ease, border-color 0.2s ease;
  }

  .part:hover {
    box-shadow: 0 14px 32px rgba(42, 47, 108, 0.07);
    border-color: rgba(42, 47, 108, 0.18);
  }

  .part-header {
    padding: 1.4rem 1.6rem 1.1rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid #f0f1f5;
  }

  .part-tag {
    display: inline-flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }

  .part-n {
    background: #2a2f6c;
    color: white;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    padding: 0.25rem 0.6rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .part-time {
    background: #f0f1f8;
    color: #2a2f6c;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    padding: 0.22rem 0.55rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
  }

  .part-lead {
    color: #555;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .part-title {
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    color: #2a2f6c;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .part-summary {
    margin: 0.6rem 0 0;
    max-width: 860px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #555;
  }

  .part-cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols, 3), 1fr);
    gap: 1px;
    background: #f0f1f5;
  }

  .col {
    background: white;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.2rem 1.4rem;
  }

  .col h4,
  .part-challenges h4 {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #f0f1f5;
  }

  .col-fail h4 { color: #b54040; border-bottom-color: rgba(181, 64, 64, 0.25); }
  .col-diag h4 { color: #b07a14; border-bottom-color: rgba(176, 122, 20, 0.25); }
  .col-fix  h4 { color: #1d7a3a; border-bottom-color: rgba(29, 122, 58, 0.25); }
  .col-info h4 { color: #2a2f6c; border-bottom-color: rgba(42, 47, 108, 0.2); }

  .col ul,
  .part-challenges ul {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .col li,
  .part-challenges li {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: #4a4a4a;
    line-height: 1.55;
    padding: 0.4rem 0 0.4rem 1.1rem;
    position: relative;
  }

  .col li::before,
  .part-challenges li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.85rem;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: #2a2f6c;
    opacity: 0.4;
  }

  .col-fail li::before { background: #b54040; }
  .col-diag li::before { background: #b07a14; }
  .col-fix  li::before { background: #1d7a3a; }

  .part-challenges {
    padding: 1.1rem 1.6rem 1.3rem;
    border-top: 1px solid #f0f1f5;
    background: #fafbff;
  }

  .part-challenges h4 { color: #5b4aa8; border-bottom-color: rgba(91, 74, 168, 0.22); }

  .part-challenges ul {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    column-gap: 2rem;
  }

  .part-challenges li::before { background: #5b4aa8; }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .part-cols { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .part-challenges ul { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .section-title { font-size: 1.9rem; }
  }
</style>
